# Framing — what to send, what to withhold
## The split
| Send | Withhold |
| --- | --- |
| The artefact itself | Your assessment of it |
| Constraints the reviewer cannot discover | Why you think it satisfies them |
| What has already been tried and failed | Which option you prefer |
| How the result will be used | The reasoning that led you here |
The rule behind the table: send what is **inaccessible** to the reviewer,
withhold what is **inferable** by it. Your conclusion is inferable — that is the
thing being tested — and supplying it replaces the test with a review of your
argument.
## Phrasings that make refutation the default
| Instead of | Ask |
| --- | --- |
| "Any issues?" | "Name the input that makes this produce a wrong result" |
| "Does this handle X?" | "Show the case where X is mishandled, or state that none exists" |
| "Thoughts?" | "Argue the strongest case against this, then say whether it survives" |
| "Is the approach sound?" | "What would have to be true for this approach to be the wrong one?" |
Each right-hand form has the same property: a reviewer that finds nothing has to
say so explicitly. "No issues" is a claim; silence is not.
## Asking for the refutation to be checkable
Request a concrete failure — an input, a sequence, a state — rather than a
category. "This may have a race condition" cannot be acted on or dismissed; "two
callers reaching line 40 with the lock released" can be run.
When a reviewer can only name a category, that is still worth having, but record
it as unverified rather than as a finding. The two decay differently: a finding
gets fixed, a category gets forgotten.
## When the reviewer is another model
It will produce a fluent answer whether or not it has grounds for one, so ask
for the evidence with the verdict — the line, the input, the run. A verdict
without evidence is not weaker evidence; it is a different kind of object, and
treating it as a weak finding is how unfounded objections get half-fixed.